Response: Thank you for this important comment. We have added a dedicated Primitive-Level Microbenchmarks subsection (Table [primitive_bench]) reporting the mean +/- SD over 20 independent runs for the dominant operations underlying query processing: a single ABSE Test call (2.480 +/- 0.045 ms), a single bilinear pairing (1.010 +/- 0.035 ms), Merkle-proof verification (7.75 +/- 1.04 us), a single protected bitmap-block operation (1.19 +/- 0.14 us), and Threshold EC-ElGamal ciphertext addition (12.18 +/- 1.17 us). Together with the reported operation counts per query configuration, this lets the total query-processing latency be reconstructed by hand from primitive costs and independently checked against Table [complexity]'s O(N_u x m_c) bound. We also clarify that the sub-millisecond figures are compiled-pairing timings (BLS12-381 via a Rust-native library, not pure-Python py_ecc) -- see the corrected Experimental Setup paragraph on the pairing backend, which directly addresses your reading of this as a plain Python/py_ecc cost.

Comment 5: The experimental setup states that the evaluated datasets contain at most 20,000 records, whereas the results section claims experiments involving up to 100,000 records. The manuscript should provide consistent dataset sizes, parameters, hardware settings, raw results, source code, and reproducible experimental scripts.
